cocoa: add a software window framebuffer

The Cocoa driver has no native window framebuffer: SDL_GetWindowSurface()
goes through the renderer-backed texture path, which needs an accelerated
renderer. Without OpenGL (or when GL is broken on a given GPU), apps that
draw through the window surface get a black window and nothing else.

Keep an RGB888 buffer per window and present it with CoreGraphics:
layer-backed views (10.8+ SDLView -wantsUpdateLayer path, everything on
10.14+) get the CGImage as the layer contents inside a no-animation
CATransaction; classic views are drawn into directly under
lockFocusIfCanDraw - the AppKit way that is also valid from a secondary
thread (mpv presents from its VO thread) - clipped to the dirty rects.
-drawRect: only repaints the buffer on AppKit-initiated redraws and sends
no expose event for that (an expose per present made mpv redraw in a loop).

SDL's generic code still tries the texture framebuffer first and only
falls back to this when no accelerated renderer exists (or when
SDL_FRAMEBUFFER_ACCELERATION is "0"), so builds with OpenGL keep their
current behavior. The state is kept in named window data and the pool
macro falls back to @autoreleasepool, so the patch applies with or without
0003-cocoa-legacy-macos.patch.
The presenting thread need not be the main thread (mpv presents from its
VO thread while its main thread runs the Cocoa runloop, which delivers
-drawRect: repaints): a lock serializes the image swap and the buffer
teardown against those repaints, and the swap never leaves the image
pointer NULL.

